  "description": "Whether you need an agile commuter, a capable grocery getter, or just a cruiser for fun and fitness, Rufus is ready!\n\nThe low slung frame when shod with fat BMX tires obviously invite you to tear around the neighborhood and camp trails. But its utility can't be ignored. While it has a nice and low standover height which is great for shorter riders and casual riders, there is amble room for a frame bag, a large front basket and rear rack. The smaller wheels also get rolling with less effort and stop easier than larger bike wheels.\n\nWhile we've designed Rufus to ride pretty much like a normal sized bike, it does have a less cumbersome length. And that means fewer dirty looks when wheeling it into an elevator or commuter train. It also takes up less space in your apartment. And when you are carry your bike up some stairs, the wheel flopping is less annoying.\n\nThe unusual geometry of the Rufus also allows the cockpit length to expand as you raise the saddle height, which (if built with the right parts) means one bike can fit a range of heights. Multiple family members could share one bike. Or you can loan it to a shorter or taller friend.\n\n[On the Blog: \"Soma Rufus Bike Check\"]\n\nInternational Standard disc brake tabs (140/160mm rotors suggested)\n\nSizes: Small, Medium and Large (Depending what type of handlebars are used, the Small can fit 4' 11\" to 5'7\", the Medium can fit 5' 3\" to 5' 11\", the Large can fit 5'6\" to 6'3\")\n\nManufactured by Blacksnow Cycles\n\nDesign note: The Medium and Large size has the dropped seatstay design (seatstay tube connects to the seat tube lower than where the top tube connects). The Small size does not.\n\nMax. chainring fit based on chainline:\n\nRear dropout has M6 upper bosses and M5 lower bosses\n\nStart with the customer – find out what they want and give it to them.",
